<h2>PrivacyFence is installed</h2>
<p>Look for the PrivacyFence icon in the menu bar — that's the companion app, your way
into Approvals and Settings without needing a sign-in link from an AI client.</p>
<p>If this is the first time PrivacyFence has been installed on this account, log out
and back in once: macOS only checks group membership at login, and the companion app
and your MCP client can't reach the daemon until that happens.</p>
<p>One step left: open one of the two Claude Desktop extensions on the PrivacyFence disk
image you opened this installer from, right next to the installer itself, to add
PrivacyFence to Claude Desktop:</p>
<p><code>__MCPB_NAME__</code> — the default. Claude Desktop may ask you before a write,
and then PrivacyFence asks too.<br>
<code>__MCPB_NO_PROMPTS_NAME__</code> — shows as “PrivacyFence (no Claude prompts)”.
Claude Desktop does not ask; PrivacyFence's own approval still does.</p>
<p>Open only one of them: with both, Claude lists every tool twice.</p>
<p>Status any time from Terminal: <code>sudo /Applications/PrivacyFenceApp.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/macos_privilege_separation.sh status</code></p>
